From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] usb: dwc3: bring the A733 up through the flattened generic glue

USB3 links at 10 Gbps on this board and still does not come up on a normal
boot: dwc3 prints "this is not a DesignWare USB3 DRD Core" and no xHCI appears.
Neither the hardware nor any register value is at fault. The controller reads
GSNPSID before it touches a PHY, and the SERDES bus gates that make that
register answer were being turned on by the combo-PHY's probe (`phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy`), whose own
comment names the phys= devlink as the thing that ordered it first. That
ordering stopped existing when the Type-C connector arrived: the connector's
ports graph closes a ring -- connector -> dwc3 -> combophy -> connector -- and
fw_devlink resolves a ring by relaxing every link inside it, the phys= link
included.

Proven twice on the 8 GiB board with a kernel built from main, 2026-07-29: re-binding dwc3
by hand after boot brings up xHCI at 10 Gbps immediately, and booting the same
Image with a dtb whose ring is cut brings it up unattended at 9.3 s. Probe
order is not a contract in Linux; the fix is to stop needing one.

`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-serdes-subsystem-clocks` turns the two gates into clocks. This patch gives the controller a driver
that takes them: 6.18's flattened glue. drivers/usb/dwc3/glue.h exports
dwc3_core_probe() and dwc3-generic-plat.c is exactly the shape needed here --
take every clock with devm_clk_bulk_get_all_enabled() regardless of name or
count, handle the reset, then hand the ready block to the core with
ignore_clocks_and_resets. The core device does not exist until the block is
live, which makes the bring-up immune to devlink cycle relaxation, module load
order and any future edge in the DT graph, rather than merely un-broken for
today's graph. Its Kconfig text invites precisely this ("Say 'Y' or 'M' here if
your platform integrates DWC3 in a similar way"), so the change to the driver
is one line in the match table.

Why a glue and not just the clock provider: dwc3 enables only clocks it knows
by name -- bus_early/bus_clk, ref, suspend, utmi, pipe (core.c:2044-2098). This
controller needs five clocks up: the two SERDES bus gates plus USB2_U2_REF,
USB2_MF and USB2_SUSPEND. Two of the five are measured on the 8 GiB board by booting the
same Image without them -- dropping MF gives "DWC3 controller soft reset failed
/ -ETIMEDOUT / -110", and so does dropping U2_REF while naming MF as ref.
SUSPEND has never been removed on a boot, and the two SERDES gates have never
existed as modelled clocks on any boot, so for those three the requirement is
reasoned from the register tables, not measured; the first board test of this
glue settles them; until then they stay
open. MF is misnamed for a dwc3 clock name (the UM calls it the USB2
Microframe clock, while bus_early is documented as an AHB/AXI bus clock in
snps,dwc3.yaml:48) and it is also indispensable, so there is no honest set of dwc3 names that covers five
clocks. utmi and pipe are not spare slots for it:
on the 6 GiB board with a live SuperSpeed link, USB2_U3_UTMI and USB2_U2_PIPE both read 0,
consistent with SUBSYS_USB3P1_BGR bits 20/21 selecting the PHY as the source.
With a bulk get, the count stops mattering and no name has to be bent.

The binding is new rather than an addition to snps,dwc3.yaml because the
compatible is ours; it refs snps,dwc3-common.yaml for everything the core reads
(the quirks, dr_mode, the connector graph) and adds only reg/interrupts/clocks/
resets.

Verified: applies at fuzz 0, builds, the new compatible is present in the built
dwc3-generic-plat object, and the observable this patch was written for is met on
hardware (the 8 GiB board, 2026-08-01): xHCI registers unattended at 10.28 s --
bus 5 and 6, "Host supports USB 3.1 Enhanced SuperSpeed", irq 448, io mem
0x06a00000 -- with no hand re-bind and no cut in the connector graph. The log
still reports "Fixed dependency cycle(s) with .../usb-pd@4e/connector", so
fw_devlink relaxes exactly as before and it no longer decides anything.

NOT verified: the SuperSpeed link. J4 was empty in every session -- a roothub
reporting 10000 is not a device. And that boot ran with a foreign module
(dwc3_sun60i_a733, not from this repo) blacklisted on the command line, because
it claims this patch's compatible and bound first; that collision needs a
decision of its own.

Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/drivers/usb/dwc3/Kconfig b/drivers/usb/dwc3/Kconfig
--- a/drivers/usb/dwc3/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc3/Kconfig
@@ -195,7 +195,7 @@
 	default USB_DWC3
 	help
 	  Support USB3 functionality in simple SoC integrations.
-	  Currently supports SpacemiT DWC USB3. Platforms using
+	  Currently supports SpacemiT and Allwinner A733 DWC USB3. Platforms using
 	  dwc3-of-simple can easily switch to dwc3-generic by flattening
 	  the dwc3 child node in the device tree.
 	  Say 'Y' or 'M' here if your platform integrates DWC3 in a similar way.
diff --git a/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-generic-plat.c b/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-generic-plat.c
--- a/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-generic-plat.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-generic-plat.c
@@ -143,6 +143,7 @@
 };
 
 static const struct of_device_id dwc3_generic_of_match[] = {
+	{ .compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-dwc3", },
 	{ .compatible = "spacemit,k1-dwc3", },
 	{ /* sentinel */ }
 };
diff --git a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/usb/allwinner,sun60i-a733-dwc3.yaml b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/usb/allwinner,sun60i-a733-dwc3.yaml
new file mode 100644
--- a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/usb/allwinner,sun60i-a733-dwc3.yaml
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/usb/allwinner,sun60i-a733-dwc3.yaml
@@ -0,0 +1,86 @@
+# SPDX-License-Identifier: (GPL-2.0 OR BSD-2-Clause)
+%YAML 1.2
+---
+$id: http://devicetree.org/schemas/usb/allwinner,sun60i-a733-dwc3.yaml#
+$schema: http://devicetree.org/meta-schemas/core.yaml#
+
+title: Allwinner A733 SuperSpeed DWC3 USB controller
+
+maintainers:
+  - DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
+
+description: |
+  The Allwinner A733 (sun60iw2) embeds a DWC3 USB IP core with a USB2.0 UTMI+
+  companion PHY and a SuperSpeed lane pair routed through the COMB0 combo-PHY
+  to a Type-C connector.
+
+  The controller is described as a flat node: the glue owns the clocks and the
+  reset, so no dwc3 child node is used.
+
+  Two of the five clocks do not come from the CCU. The AHB and AXI bus clocks of
+  the USB3 block are gated inside the SERDES subsystem page
+  (allwinner,sun60i-a733-serdes-ccu), and the controller cannot even be
+  identified while they are off.
+
+allOf:
+  - $ref: snps,dwc3-common.yaml#
+
+properties:
+  compatible:
+    const: allwinner,sun60i-a733-dwc3
+
+  reg:
+    maxItems: 1
+
+  interrupts:
+    maxItems: 1
+
+  clocks:
+    items:
+      - description: USB2 reference clock
+      - description: USB2 microframe clock
+      - description: suspend clock
+      - description: USB3 AXI bus clock
+      - description: USB3 AHB bus clock
+
+  clock-names:
+    items:
+      - const: ref
+      - const: mf
+      - const: suspend
+      - const: aclk
+      - const: hclk
+
+  resets:
+    maxItems: 1
+    description: USB3 controller reset
+
+required:
+  - compatible
+  - reg
+  - interrupts
+  - clocks
+  - clock-names
+  - resets
+  - phys
+  - phy-names
+
+unevaluatedProperties: false
+
+examples:
+  - |
+    #include <dt-bindings/interrupt-controller/arm-gic.h>
+
+    usb@6a00000 {
+        compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-dwc3";
+        reg = <0x06a00000 0x10000>;
+        interrupts = <GIC_SPI 155 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+        clocks = <&ccu 49>, <&ccu 51>, <&ccu 50>,
+                 <&serdes_ccu 0>, <&serdes_ccu 1>;
+        clock-names = "ref", "mf", "suspend", "aclk", "hclk";
+        resets = <&ccu 20>;
+        phys = <&u2phy>, <&combophy0>;
+        phy-names = "usb2-phy", "usb3-phy";
+        dr_mode = "host";
+        maximum-speed = "super-speed-plus";
+    };
